.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
User authentication (RFC 4252, RFC 4256), as client and server state machines.
.PP
They sit on top of the transport (conn.h) but do not depend on it.  The application routes the messages numbered 50 to 79 that gf_conn delivers to gf_auth_server_input or gf_auth_client_input, and each machine sends its own through a send callback, normally gf_conn_send.  Time is a caller\-supplied clock in microseconds, as everywhere.
.PP
Methods:
.PP
.nf
.RS
none                  only to learn the methods a server offers
publickey             RFC 4252 section 7: the client first asks
                      whether a key would be accepted, then signs;
                      the signature covers the session identifier
publickey\-hostbound\-v00@openssh.com
                      the same, with the server's host key in the
                      signed data, so that a signature made for one
                      server cannot be replayed to another by a
                      forwarding agent
password              RFC 4252 section 8, without password change
keyboard\-interactive  RFC 4256
.RE
.fi
.PP
Certificates (key.h): a client may present an OpenSSH user certificate in place of a plain key.  A server accepts one only if its config names the CAs it trusts (trusted_user_cas); without any, certificate signature algorithms are refused.  Before the application sees the request, the machine checks that a trusted CA signed it, that it is a user certificate good at the current time, that the user name is one of its principals, that its "source\-address" (if any) allows the client's address, and that every critical option is one it understands, and the application is told what it must itself honour: the "force\-command" option is in the request and is kept for gf_auth_server_force_command. A key revocation list in the configuration (krl) is consulted for every key, certificate or not; anything else the application knows about revocation (gf_key_certificate_info has the serial and key id) is its decision.
.PP
X.509 chains (RFC 6187, x509.h) are accepted the same way if the server's configuration has a trust store (x509_trust): the machine checks the chain against it for a user (id\-kp\-secureShellClient), at the current time, before the application sees the request, which is then marked x509 and left to the application to map to a user from the subject (gf_x509_subject, gf_x509_san).  Without a trust store no chain is accepted.
.PP
Security keys (FIDO, key.h) are accepted as public keys: the machine verifies the token's signature, requires the user presence flag, and hands the application the flags and counter.  A client signs with one through the sign event, the application driving the token (the library does not talk to tokens).
.PP
Not implemented: hostbased (it trusts a client machine's identity on the word of its administrator and the DNS), and password change (a request to change the password is refused).  A signature algorithm is only ever one of policy.h: a client cannot sign, and a server will not accept, an ssh\-rsa signature.
.PP
The server's side is asynchronous.  A request is handed to the application, which decides now or later and answers with one of gf_auth_server_accept, _reject, _pk_ok or _kbdint_prompt.  Everything that can be checked without the application is checked first: the encoding, the algorithm against the key, the key against the policy, and the signature.  The application therefore only sees signed requests whose signature is valid, and only has to say whether it trusts the key for the user.
.PP
Limits: a server ends the connection after max_failures failed attempts (default 6; a "none" request and a public key query are not attempts), and after a grace time (default 120 s) without success.  A client gives up after max_attempts requests.
